From the source code, I obtained information on:

  Trap detection
  Crystal balls

I'm posting this in response to the question of what does the
message "Your toes stop itching" mean?

SPOILER ALERT


There are several ways of detecting all the traps -- traps in the
floor, traps on doors and traps on chests -- on a level.  These will
be mentioned below.

When such trap detection takes place, if there are no traps at all on
the level, you'll get the message "Your toes stop itching."  If there
is only one trap on the level, and you are standing on it, you'll get
the message "Your toes itch."  Otherwise, you'll be shown where the
traps are.

Reading a blessed or uncursed scroll of gold detection while confused
will result in trap detection instead of gold detection.  Reading a
cursed scroll of gold detection (whether confused or not) will perform
trap detection, except that $ symbols will be displayed instead of ^
symbols.  (Note: This differs somewhat from what the WCST spoiler file
says.)

A crystal ball can be used to perform trap detection.  Just type ^ or
"trap" when asked "What do you want to look for?"

In general, a crystal ball can (only) perform the following five
functions:

Gold detection -- type $ or "gold" when prompted.
Trap detection -- type ^ or "traps".
Food detection -- type % or "food".  All food type items (food
                  rations, corpses, tripe, etc.) will be displayed.
Object detection -- type any of the symbols ! * ) ( / ? + [ or type 
                  "objects".  All objects (not just the symbol
                  entered) will be detected just like using a potion
                  of object detection.
Monster detection -- type something other than the above (but don't
                  just hit return); try typing "monsters".  Works
                  like a potion of monster detection.

Just hitting a carriage return when prompted what to look for cancels
the use of the crystal ball without using up a "charge".

If a crystal ball is cursed, or if a roll of a twenty-sided dice is
greater that your current intelligence, then a crystal ball may (with
equal proability):

Do nothing.  You get the message "The crystal ball is too much to
  comprehend!".  One "charge" of the crystal ball is used.
Make you confused for 1-100 turns.  You get the message "The crystal
  ball confuses you!".  Again, one charge is used.
Make you blind for 1-100 turns.  You get "The crystal ball damages
  your vision!", and one charge is used.
Make you hallucinate for 1-100 turns.  The message is "The crystal
  ball zaps your mind!", and one change is used.
BLOW UP! causing 1-30 points of damage.  "The crystal ball explodes!",
  and obviously the ball is gone.

You'll get the message "The vision is unclear." if the crystal ball is
out of charges, or if no objects of the type requested are detected. 

Finally, if no objects of the type requested are found, there is a 1%
chance of getting the message "You see the Wizard of Yendor gazing out
at you." instead of the "The vision is unclear." message.  Ignore it;
it's just for fun. 



